:: GiveStoneGift [childhood]
# A Token of Respect

From your collection of stones, you select the most beautiful one - a small piece of quartz that catches the light and seems to glow from within. You hold it out to your grandfather.

"For you," you say simply.

Your grandfather's eyes widen with surprise, then crinkle with pleasure. He takes the stone carefully, examining it in the sunlight.

"This is a special stone," he says, his voice warm with appreciation. "It holds the light of the sun inside it."

He explains that such stones are rare and valued by the tribe. Some believe they have special powers - to protect from harm, to bring good dreams, or to help the shaman communicate with spirits.

"I will keep this as a treasure," your grandfather tells you, tucking the stone into a small pouch he wears around his neck. "And whenever I look at it, I will think of you and your generous heart."

The simple act of giving has created a stronger bond between you and your grandfather. You begin to understand that gifts are not just about the objects themselves, but about the connections they create between people.

